# The Convex deployment this checkout talks to. `npx convex dev` writes
# CONVEX_DEPLOYMENT and NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL; the site URL is the same
# deployment on its .convex.site domain, where the auth routes live.
CONVEX_DEPLOYMENT=dev:your-deployment
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL=https://your-deployment.convex.cloud
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_SITE_URL=https://your-deployment.convex.site

# These live on the Convex deployment itself, not in this file:
#   npx convex env set SITE_URL http://localhost:3000
#   npx convex env set BETTER_AUTH_SECRET "$(openssl rand -base64 32)"
#
# And these two carry the mill's email, which is how an Admin invites an
# Operatore and how a forgotten password is replaced (ADR-0008). RESEND_FROM
# has to be an address on a domain the Resend account has verified; without it
# the app writes from Resend's own test sender.
#   npx convex env set RESEND_API_KEY re_...
#   npx convex env set RESEND_FROM "Pendolino Ceste <ceste@frantoio.example>"
#
# And this one says the deployment is only pretending. On true, an invitation
# is written and its message built, but nothing is handed to Resend: the link
# goes to the Convex logs, where whoever is trying the flow reads it, and the
# Operatori screen says the email was not sent. Set it on a deployment somebody
# is working against, so that trying an invitation does not put a real message
# in somebody's inbox. Anything other than true or false is refused.
#   npx convex env set RESEND_TEST_MODE true
#
# With no RESEND_API_KEY the counter works exactly as it does with one: nothing
# on the deployment sends mail, and an invitation shows on the Operatori screen
# as one that never went out, which is where a Resend failure is meant to land.

# And these carry the mill's SMS: the receipt a Ritiro and a Rientro send to
# the Cliente, and the messages an Admin writes by hand. They live on the
# Convex deployment, like the Resend keys above, and never in the database.
# Sending is done with an API key against the account: the key is revoked and
# reissued from the Twilio console in a minute, where the account's auth token
# opens the account itself. The auth token is set all the same and does one job
# — Twilio signs its delivery callbacks with it and with nothing else, so a
# deployment without it sends every message and never learns which arrived.
# The sender the Cliente sees is NOT here: it is the Mittente, settled by an
# Admin on the Frantoio screen and kept in the database (ADR-0011). These three
# are secrets because leaking one costs money; the Mittente is read off every
# telephone the mill writes to, and a mill should be able to correct its own
# name without a deploy.
#   npx convex env set TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID AC...
#   npx convex env set TWILIO_API_KEY_SID SK...
#   npx convex env set TWILIO_API_KEY_SECRET ...
#   npx convex env set TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN ...
#
# And this one says the deployment is only pretending. On true, the Sms is
# written down and its words built, but nothing is handed to Twilio: the
# message goes to the Convex logs and the row says it was not sent. Set it on
# a deployment somebody is working against, so that trying a Ritiro does not
# put a real message on a real farmer's telephone. Anything other than true or
# false is refused.
#   npx convex env set TWILIO_TEST_MODE true
#
# With no Twilio account the counter works exactly as it does with one: the
# Ritiro is registered, the Sms is written down, and the row says it did not go
# out. Nothing at the counter ever waits on a carrier (ADR-0005).
